  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "If $\\alpha \\beta =-5$ and $a\\cdot b=3$, then what is $(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-9?$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in both variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-9 & =\\alpha (b\\cdot \\beta a)-9 \\\\  & =\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using that the dot product is symmetric, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-9 & =\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\alpha \\beta $ and $a\\cdot b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-9 & =(-5)\\cdot (3)-9 \\\\  & =-15-9 \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$",
